e from our notes. We have facts for all candidates. We should perhaps discuss Candidate B's strengths and concerns, or compare C vs B, etc. We need to make a focused argumentative contribution, include our current recommendation (vote). We can either keep vote for C or shift.\n\nWe need to consider the criteria. Let's evaluate each candidate with our notes:\n\nCandidate A:\n- anticipates dangerous situations (good decision quality, safety)\n- sees complex connections (technical competence)\n- excellent spatial vision (technical)\n- very good leadership qualities (crew cooperation, responsibility)\n- regarded as a show-off (possible crew cooperation issue)\n- not open to new ideas (adaptability)\n\nNo info on reliability, stress resilience, memory, communication.\n\nCandidate B:\n- very conscientious (reliability)\n- handles stress very well (stress resilience)\n- good at assessing weather conditions (technical competence)\n- excellent computer skills (technical)\n- relatively weak memory for numbers (attention/accuracy)\n- makes nasty remarks about his colleagues (crew cooperation, professional communication)\n\nCandidate C:\n- can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality)\n- keeps calm in a crisis (stress resilience)\n- understands complicated technology (technical)\n- difficulty communicating ideas (communication)\n- regarded as egocentric (crew cooperation)\n- not very willing to further his education (adaptability)\n\nCandidate D:\n- responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience)\n- can concentrate very well (attention)\n- solves problems extremely well (technical/problem solving)\n- takes responsibility seriously (responsibility)\n- regarded as a know-it-all (crew cooperation)\n- has a hot temper (crew cooperation, professionalism)\n\nNow, Agent 1's message: they gave strengths: 100% reliable, positive atmosphere, correct decisions quickly. Concerns: communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. They voted for C.\n\nWe need to respond. Perhaps we can argue that while C has reliability, his communication issues are serious for cockpit, and B's stress resilience and conscientiousness may outweigh. Or we could bring up Candidate B's strengths and concerns, compare to C. Or we could bring up Candidate A's anticipation and leadership.\n\nWe need to keep the discussion systematic, covering other candidates. So we could say: I see the strengths of C, but I have concerns about his communication and adaptability, which are critical. I think Candidate B's strong stress handling and conscientiousness make him a strong alternative, though his nasty remarks are concerning. I propose we discuss B next.\n\nOur vote could be still C, or shift to B. Since we need to give a vote now, we could keep provisional vote for C but note we need to evaluate B. Or we could change to B. The guidelines: \"Your vote is your current provisional recommendation, not a fixed position.\" So we can change if we think B is better.\n\nGiven the evidence, C's communication difficulty is a serious safety issue. B also has communication issues (nasty remarks). Both have concerns. Which is more severe? Communication difficulty vs nasty remarks. Both affect crew cooperation. B also has weak memory for numbers, which could affect attention. C has not shown memory issues. C's adaptability is low (not willing to further education). B's adaptability unknown. B's stress resilience is high. C also calm in crisis.\n\nOverall, maybe C still best because reliability and decision quality are top. But we need to discuss others.\n\nThus we can keep vote for C, but we need to bring up B's facts and suggest we discuss B next. That would be a focused contribution: agree with Agent 1 on C's strengths, but highlight concerns, and propose to examine B.\n\nWe must include explicit candidate facts as evidence. Provide at least one fact for each candidate we discuss.\n\nThus public message: \"I agree with Agent 1 that Candidate C's reliability and decision speed are strong, but his communication difficulty and egocentric attitude raise concerns. Candidate B, on the other hand, is very conscientious (reliability) and handles stress well (stress resilience), but makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation) and has weak memory for numbers (attention). Given the trade\u2011offs, I still provisionally vote for Candidate C, but I think we should now examine Candidate B's profile in more detail to see if his strengths outweigh his concerns.\"\n\nWe need to include vote in metadata: \"Candidate C\".\n\nLet's produce that."}
